{"ts": "2026-08-17T12:44:00Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 13, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "1\t# Spec: Give tasks a due date\n2\t\n3\t**Taiga:** #12\n4\t**Type:** Feature\n5\t**Branch:** feature/tp-12/give-tasks-due-date\n6\t\n7\t*Note: this overwrites `docs/spec.md` and `docs/design.md` from the previous\n8\tcycle (Taiga #11, search tasks by title), which is already merged (`7c1276f`\n9\t/ `5e852f6`, working tree clean at dispatch time). That's expected \u2014 `docs/`\n10\tis scratch state scoped to the current cycle, not permanent documentation.*\n11\t\n12\t## Goal\n13\t\n14\tLet me set an optional due date on a task, see it on the task in the list,\n15\tand be able to tell an overdue task apart from the rest without relying on\n16\tcolour alone.\n17\t\n18\t## Acceptance criteria\n19\t\n20\t1. **Given** I am creating a new task, **when** I submit it without touching\n21\t   the due-date field, **then** the task is created with no due date \u2014 due\n22\t   date is optional, never required.\n23\t2. **Given** I am creating a new task, **when** I set a due date before\n24\t   submitting, **then** the created task carries that due date and it is\n25\t   visible on the task row in the list.\n26\t3. **Given** an existing task (with or without a due date), **when** I set,\n27\t   change, or clear its due date from the task row, **then** the task's due\n28\t   date updates accordingly \u2014 clearing must be possible, not just setting\n29\t   (this mirrors priority, which is editable per-row via\n30\t   `frontend/src/components/TaskList.jsx:100-107`, but due date must also\n31\t   support going back to \"none\").\n32\t4. **Given** a task's due date is strictly before today's date, **when** the\n33\t   list renders, **then** that task is visibly marked overdue by something\n34\t   other than colour alone \u2014 e.g. a text label or icon with accessible text\n35\t   \u2014 so the distinction doesn't depend on colour perception. Exact visual\n36\t   treatment is the ux-designer's call.\n37\t5. **Given** a task's due date is today, **when** the list renders, **then**\n38\t   it is **not** marked overdue \u2014 \"overdue\" means the date has passed, not\n39\t   that it's due today. (Open Question 1 below.)\n40\t6. **Given** a task's due date is in the future, or the task has no due date\n41\t   at all, **when** the list renders, **then** it is not marked overdue.\n42\t7. **Given** an overdue task is marked done, **when** the list renders,\n43\t   **then** it no longer shows the overdue indicator \u2014 completion suppresses\n44\t   it regardless of the stored due date. (Open Question 2 below.)\n45\t8. **Given** I submit a malformed due date value to the API directly (not\n46\t   reachable through the date-picker UI, but reachable via direct API use),\n47\t   **when** the request is validated, **then** it is rejected with `422`\n48\t   and the task's stored due date is unchanged.\n49\t9. **Given** the backend restarts, **when** it reloads its snapshot,\n50\t   **then** each task's due date (set or absent) survives, the same way\n51\t   `priority` already does (`backend/tests/test_persistence.py:6-20`).\n52\t\n53\t## Non-goals\n54\t\n55\t- No sorting or filtering by due date, or by overdue status. The request\n56\t  asks only that overdue tasks *stand out visually* in the existing list \u2014\n57\t  it does not ask to reorder the list or add a due-date filter alongside\n58\t  the existing state filter (`frontend/src/components/TaskFilter.jsx`) and\n59\t  search (`frontend/src/components/SearchTasks.jsx`). The existing\n60\t  high-priority-first sort (`frontend/src/App.jsx:42-44`) is untouched.\n61\t- No due *time* \u2014 a due date is a calendar date only, no time-of-day\n62\t  component. \"Overdue\" is a whole-day comparison against today's date.\n63\t- No reminders, notifications, or scheduled jobs of any kind.\n64\t- No recurring/repeating due dates.\n65\t- No timezone handling beyond the server's local date. This is a small\n66\t  in-memory demo app with a single backend instance; per-user timezones are\n67\t  out of scope, same spirit as the rest of the store.\n68\t\n69\t## Affected areas\n70\t\n71\t**Backend**\n72\t- `backend/app/models.py` \u2014 add `due_date: date | None = None` to `TaskIn`\n73\t  and `Task` (mirrors how `priority: Priority` sits on both, lines 10-11 and\n74\t  21-22); add a `DueDateIn` model (mirrors `PriorityIn`, lines 14-15) whose\n75\t  `due_date` field is `date | None` so it can carry an explicit \"clear\"\n76\t  value, not just a new date.\n77\t- `backend/app/main.py` \u2014 `create_task` (lines 27-29) already passes\n78\t  `payload` fields through `TaskIn`, so due date on create needs no new\n79\t  route, only the model change above; add\n80\t  `PATCH /api/tasks/{task_id}/due-date` (mirrors `update_priority`,\n81\t  lines 40-45) taking `DueDateIn` and calling a new store method.\n82\t- `backend/app/store.py` \u2014 `add()` (line 54) gains a `due_date` parameter\n83\t  defaulting to `None`; add `set_due_date(task_id, due_date)` (mirrors\n84\t  `set_priority`, lines 72-78), where `due_date=None` is a valid value that\n85\t  clears it. `_save()` (lines 44-49) needs no change \u2014 `model_dump()`\n86\t  already picks up any new `Task` field automatically.\n87\t- `backend/tests/test_tasks.py` \u2014 new tests following the existing\n88\t  priority tests (lines 85-133) for: create without due date defaults to\n89\t  `None`, create with an explicit due date, update due date, clear an\n90\t  existing due date (set to `null`), invalid due date rejected with `422`\n91\t  and storage left unchanged, updating due date of a missing task is\n92\t  `404`, updating title/priority leaves due date untouched and vice versa.\n93\t- `backend/tests/test_persistence.py` \u2014 extend the restart test (lines\n94\t  6-20), following the existing `priority=\"high\"` pattern, to also set and\n95\t  assert a due date survives a reload.\n96\t\n97\t**Frontend**\n98\t- `frontend/src/api.js` \u2014 `createTask` (lines 18-19) gains a due-date\n99\t  argument passed through in the POST body; add a `setDueDate(id, dueDate)`\n100\t  wrapper hitting the new `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}/due-date` (mirrors\n101\t  `setPriority`, lines 23-24).\n102\t- `frontend/src/components/AddTask.jsx` \u2014 add a due-date input alongside\n103\t  the existing priority `<select>` (lines 30-37), included in the `onAdd`\n104\t  call (line 17) and reset after submit (line 19), same lifecycle as\n105\t  `priority` in this component.\n106\t- `frontend/src/components/TaskList.jsx` \u2014 display each task's due date on\n107\t  its row and make it editable, following the same per-row pattern as the\n108\t  priority `<select>` (lines 100-107); render the overdue indicator per AC4\n109\t  based on comparing `task.due_date` to today, suppressed when `task.done`\n110\t  per AC7; wire a new `onDueDateChange` prop through from `App.jsx`.\n111\t- `frontend/src/App.jsx` \u2014 pass a due date through to\n112\t  `api.createTask` in the `AddTask onAdd` handler (line 64); add an\n113\t  `onDueDateChange` handler calling `api.setDueDate` (mirrors\n114\t  `onPriorityChange`, line 84) and pass it to `TaskList`.\n115\t- `frontend/src/App.css` \u2014 styling for the new due-date input/display and\n116\t  the overdue indicator (colour *plus* the non-colour cue from AC4);\n117\t  exact treatment is the ux-designer's call, following the existing\n118\t  `.priority-select` rules (lines 96-116) as the nearest precedent for a\n119\t  per-row status affordance.\n120\t\n121\t## Edge cases\n122\t\n123\t- **No due date set:** never shows as overdue, at any point (AC6).\n124\t- **Due date exactly today:** not overdue (AC5) \u2014 this is the one boundary\n125\t  condition worth a dedicated test on both sides (yesterday overdue, today\n126\t  not).\n127\t- **Due date far in the past vs. one day past:** both overdue; no\n128\t  \"how overdue\" gradation is in scope.\n129\t- **Overdue task gets marked done:** indicator disappears (AC7).\n130\t- **Done task's due date is edited to a past date:** stays without the\n131\t  overdue indicator \u2014 same rule as AC7, done always suppresses it,\n132\t  regardless of the order the two edits happen in.\n133\t- **Task un-done after being completed while overdue:** no explicit\n134\t  \"un-complete\" action exists in this app today (only `onComplete`,\n135\t  `App.jsx:81`, one-way to done) \u2014 not reachable, no criterion needed.\n136\t- **Clearing a due date that was never set:** a no-op, not an error.\n137\t- **Malformed due date string via direct API call:** `422`, existing task\n138\t  data untouched (AC8) \u2014 same shape as the existing invalid-priority test\n139\t  (`test_updating_priority_with_invalid_value_is_rejected`,\n140\t  `backend/tests/test_tasks.py:122-126`).\n141\t- **Due date combined with search/state filter:** due date is just another\n142\t  field on the row; it has no interaction with the existing filter\n143\t  (`TaskFilter.jsx`) or search (`SearchTasks.jsx`) \u2014 both keep filtering on\n144\t  `done`/`title` exactly as today.\n145\t\n146\t## Open questions\n147\t\n148\t1. **Does \"overdue\" mean strictly before today, or does it include today?**\n149\t   Proceeding under: strictly before today (AC5) \u2014 \"overdue\" colloquially\n150\t   means the date has passed, not that it's due today. If the intent was\n151\t   \"flag it today too,\" that's a one-line comparison change\n152\t   (`<` vs `<=`) in the frontend overdue check, not a re-architecture.\n153\t2. **Should completing a task suppress its overdue indicator?**\n154\t   Proceeding under: yes (AC7) \u2014 a done task reads as finished regardless\n155\t   of when it was finished; leaving a red \"overdue\" badge on a completed\n156\t   task would look like unresolved work. If the intent is to keep showing\n157\t   overdue status on done tasks too (e.g. to see it was late), that's also\n158\t   a small, contained change to the same display condition.\n159\t", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_018M5kZ8Snpm7k3jQVzo1bbs", "session_id": "94263fb4-db33-4b3e-80bf-f747e45804ae"}}
